# Jira Plugin Integration (New Frontend System)

## What this achieves
Shows a live Jira Overview card (issue counts, ticket table, activity stream) on a component's entity page in Backstage, pulled directly from a real Jira Cloud project.

## Important context
The official Roadie Jira plugin guide assumes the **old/legacy** Backstage frontend system (editing `packages/app/src/components/catalog/EntityPage.tsx`). This app uses the **new frontend system** (`createApp` from `@backstage/frontend-defaults`), where entity page content is registered as extensions instead. This guide adapts the plugin using that pattern — this same approach works for adapting most other legacy-only community plugins.

## Prerequisites
- A working Backstage app on the new frontend system
- A component already registered in the catalog (e.g. via GitHub, with a `catalog-info.yaml`)
- A Jira Cloud account with a project

---

## Part 1 — Install the plugin
```powershell
yarn --cwd packages/app add @roadiehq/backstage-plugin-jira
```

## Part 2 — Get Jira credentials

1. Note your Jira Cloud URL, e.g. `https://yourcompany.atlassian.net`
2. Create an API token: **https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens** → "Create API token" → copy it immediately
3. Build the Basic-auth string in PowerShell:
```powershell
$pair = "youremail@example.com:YOUR_API_TOKEN"
$bytes = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes($pair)
$encoded = [Convert]::ToBase64String($bytes)
Write-Output "Basic $encoded"
```
4. Set it as an environment variable in the terminal you'll run `yarn start` from:
```powershell
$env:JIRA_TOKEN="Basic <the encoded string>"
```
(This only lasts for the current terminal session — set it again each time you open a new one, or use a persistent secrets approach for anything beyond local testing.)

## Part 3 — Config in `app-config.yaml`

```yaml
proxy:
  '/jira/api':
    target: 'https://yourcompany.atlassian.net'
    headers:
      Authorization: ${JIRA_TOKEN}
      Accept: 'application/json'
      Content-Type: 'application/json'
      X-Atlassian-Token: 'nocheck'
      User-Agent: "MY-UA-STRING"

jira:
  proxyPath: /jira/api
  product: cloud   # 'cloud' or 'datacenter'

backend:
  csp:
    img-src:
      - "'self'"
      - 'data:'
      - 'https://yourcompany.atlassian.net'
```

## Part 4 — Entity card: `packages/app/src/catalog/JiraCard/index.tsx`

> Note the `.tsx` extension — required because this file contains JSX. A `.ts` extension will fail to compile.

```tsx
import { EntityCardBlueprint } from '@backstage/plugin-catalog-react/alpha';
import { compatWrapper } from '@backstage/core-compat-api';
import React from 'react';
import { EntityJiraOverviewCard, isJiraAvailable } from '@roadiehq/backstage-plugin-jira';

export const jiraOverviewCard = EntityCardBlueprint.make({
  name: 'jira-overview',
  params: {
    filter: isJiraAvailable,
    loader: async () =>
      compatWrapper(<EntityJiraOverviewCard />),
  },
});
```
`compatWrapper` bridges a legacy-style plugin component (built for the old `EntitySwitch`/`EntityPage.tsx` system) into the new frontend system.

## Part 5 — Utility API: `packages/app/src/apis.ts`

The plugin's `JiraAPI` class needs four dependencies to construct: `discoveryApi`, `configApi`, `fetchApi`, `errorApi`.

```typescript
import {
  ApiBlueprint,
  discoveryApiRef,
  configApiRef,
  fetchApiRef,
  errorApiRef,
} from '@backstage/frontend-plugin-api';
import { jiraApiRef, JiraAPI } from '@roadiehq/backstage-plugin-jira';

export const jiraApi = ApiBlueprint.make({
  name: 'jira',
  params: define =>
    define({
      api: jiraApiRef,
      deps: {
        discoveryApi: discoveryApiRef,
        configApi: configApiRef,
        fetchApi: fetchApiRef,
        errorApi: errorApiRef,
      },
      factory: ({ discoveryApi, configApi, fetchApi, errorApi }) =>
        new JiraAPI({ discoveryApi, configApi, fetchApi, errorApi }),
    }),
});
```

If a plugin's constructor requirements aren't documented, check them directly:
```powershell
Get-Content "node_modules\@roadiehq\backstage-plugin-jira\dist\api\index.esm.js" | Select-Object -First 40
```

## Part 6 — Wire both into `packages/app/src/App.tsx`

```tsx
import { createApp } from '@backstage/frontend-defaults';
import catalogPlugin from '@backstage/plugin-catalog/alpha';
import { navModule } from './modules/nav';

import { githubAuthApiRef } from '@backstage/core-plugin-api';
import { SignInPageBlueprint } from '@backstage/plugin-app-react';
import { SignInPage } from '@backstage/core-components';
import { createFrontendModule } from '@backstage/frontend-plugin-api';

import { OrgPickerFieldExtension } from './scaffolder/OrgPicker';
import { TeamPickerFieldExtension } from './scaffolder/TeamPicker';
import { jiraOverviewCard } from './catalog/JiraCard';
import { jiraApi } from './apis';

const signInPage = SignInPageBlueprint.make({
  params: {
    loader: async () => props =>
      (
        <SignInPage
          {...props}
          provider={{
            id: 'github-auth-provider',
            title: 'GitHub',
            message: 'Sign in using GitHub',
            apiRef: githubAuthApiRef,
          }}
        />
      ),
  },
});

const scaffolderFieldExtensions = createFrontendModule({
  pluginId: 'scaffolder',
  extensions: [OrgPickerFieldExtension, TeamPickerFieldExtension],
});

const jiraModule = createFrontendModule({
  pluginId: 'catalog',
  extensions: [jiraOverviewCard, jiraApi],
});

export default createApp({
  features: [
    catalogPlugin,
    navModule,
    createFrontendModule({
      pluginId: 'app',
      extensions: [signInPage],
    }),
    scaffolderFieldExtensions,
    jiraModule,
  ],
});
```

## Part 7 — Annotate the target component

In the component's `catalog-info.yaml` (in its own repo, alongside its code):
```yaml
metadata:
  annotations:
    jira/project-key: YOUR_PROJECT_KEY   # e.g. the prefix on ticket IDs, like JMS in JMS-42
```
Commit and push this file to the component's repo — if the component was registered via a GitHub URL (`type: url` in `catalog.locations`), Backstage reads this file live from GitHub, not from any local copy.

## Part 8 — Refresh and verify

1. Restart `yarn start` (in the same terminal where `$env:JIRA_TOKEN` is set)
2. Go to the component's entity page → "..." menu → "Schedule entity refresh" (or wait a few minutes for the automatic catalog refresh)
3. Confirm the annotation landed:
   ```
   http://localhost:7007/api/catalog/entities/by-name/component/default/<component-name>
   ```
   Check the JSON for `"jira/project-key"`.
4. Open the component's Overview tab — the Jira card should now show live issue counts, the ticket table, and the activity stream.

## The general pattern (reusable for other legacy-only plugins)
1. Install the package as usual.
2. Add its proxy/config to `app-config.yaml` (backend-level config is usually unaffected by frontend system version).
3. Wrap its entity component with `EntityCardBlueprint.make()` + `compatWrapper()`, instead of editing `EntityPage.tsx`.
4. If it exposes a Utility API (`xApiRef` + a client class), register it with `ApiBlueprint.make()`, checking the actual installed source for the exact constructor dependencies.
5. Register both extensions in `App.tsx` inside a `createFrontendModule` targeting the right `pluginId` (`catalog` for entity cards/APIs, `scaffolder` for template fields, etc.)

## Key takeaway
> Any Backstage plugin built for the legacy frontend system can still be used in a new-frontend-system app — it just needs to be wrapped in `EntityCardBlueprint`/`ApiBlueprint` + `compatWrapper`, rather than plugged into `EntityPage.tsx`.
